n: 3.12.2.4
name: second-class-base-is-flattening
kind: finding
is: For a second class base the 2026 body reaches the thesis behaviour by flattening: the base's method runs on the same receiver record, its fields are slots of that record, and dispatch by the record's class id lets the derived override answer. No base object exists, the two bases cannot differ, and a base cannot be shared or supplied at creation.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.12.2.1 3.12.2.2
see: 2.20.16#234
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-object-model-witness

n: 3.12.2.5
name: delegate-field-loses-self
kind: finding
is: For a field marked delegate the 2026 lowering makes the delegate object the head of the packed list, so inside the delegate's method both bases are the delegate; a delegate method that calls a method the outer class overrides runs the delegate's own. The 2000 rule, keep self and move only the structural base, is not preserved.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.12.2.2 3.12.2.3 3.12.1.3
limit: Behaviour inferred from source. No fixture in the head tree or the tree before the cut of 2026-09-29 tests it: the two fixtures that existed for the path forward a method returning a constant (answer 10).
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-object-model-witness

n: 3.12.3
name: templates
kind: hub
is: Templates in both eras are specialisation by substitution, and both have a record of running: 2000 instance class files named Name[args] are compiled and named in load logs; the 2026 fixture bml-class-templates.bml (answer 133) is under the pinned Verdict 26. Parameter bounds were syntax in 2000, and the 2026 grammar keeps parameter names only; neither era checks them. The 2026 maintenance dialect reads generic class headers and erases the type parameter.
